  * `P` - Pawn\n        * Color (Player):\n            * `W` - White (moves first)\n            * `B` - Black\n        * 2Step (En Passant flag)\n            * `F` - Did NOT move 2 squares last turn\n            * `T` - Moved 2 squares last turn (en passant eligible)\n        * Moved (Movement flag)\n            * `1` - Has moved before\n            * `0` - Has NOT moved yet\n        \n        ### Examples: \n            * PWF1: White Pawn, not available for en passant, already moved; \n            * RBF0：Black Rook, never moved (castling eligible);\n            * KWT1: White king, irrelevant 2-step flag, has moved;\n            * QWF0 = White queen, irrelevant 2-step flag, never moved.\n\n        Now is your turn, to your best ability, choose your move to maximize your chances of winning.\n        Please briefly give a overview of the situation, evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output one of the valid actions with specified format.\n        Your response format should be:\n        Situation: [Your Observation]\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\n        ', 'user_prompt': 'You are playing as Black.\nCurrent state representation is as follows:\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n8 |RBF0|NBF0|BBF0|QBF0|KBF0|BBF0|NBF0|RBF0| 8\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n7 |PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|    |PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|PBF0| 7\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n6 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 6\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n5 |    |    |    |PBT1|    |    |    |    | 5\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n4 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 4\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n3 |PWF1|    |    |    |    |NWF1|    |    | 3\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n2 |    |PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0| 2\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|BWF0|QWF0|KWF0|BWF0|    |RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, N: b8-a6, N: b8-c6, N: b8-d7, P: c7-c5, P: c7-c6, B: c8-d7, B: c8-e6, B: c8-f5, B: c8-g4, B: c8-h3, P: d5-d4, Q: d8-d7, Q: d8-d6, P: e7-e5, P: e7-e6, K: e8-d7, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, N: g8-f6, N: g8-h6, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: I am playing as Black, and the board is in the early-mid game phase. White has advanced a pawn to a3 (PWF1 on a3), likely pressuring my queenside. My central pawns are mostly undeveloped, with only the d5-pawn having moved (PBT1). There is an isolated white knight on f3 (NWF1), which may be targeting g5 or e5. My king is safe, and both sides have castling rights intact. However, White has not yet disturbed their kingside structure.\n\nThe center is partially open, especially around d5 and e5, offering opportunities for counterplay. The d-file is semi-open, and my queen on d8 can exert pressure if developed properly. Several of my pieces are still on starting squares (knights on b8/g8, bishop on c8, etc.), so development is a key priority.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: d5-d4 – Pushing the d5 pawn challenges the center immediately and gains space. It opens lines for my dark-squared bishop and queen. This is a strong, aggressive move that fights for control.\n- N: g8-f6 – Developing the knight to f6 targets White’s e4 (if they play it) and supports e5/d4 control. It\'s a classical developing move, harmonious with opening principles.\n- P: e7-e5 – A central break that mirrors P:d5-d4. However, it could weaken d5 if not supported, and it doesn’t directly respond to White’s presence on a3.\n- Q: d8-d6 – Moves the queen early to target h2 or support an attack, but risks being harassed by minor pieces; premature queen development.\n- B: c8-g4 – Pins a potential knight on f3, but currently there\'s no piece on f3 to pin (White has a knight on f3? Wait — yes! NWF1 on f6? No — wait: NWF1 is on f3? Let\'s check: f3 is empty. NWF1 is on f3? No — looking back: NWF1 is on f3? In the board: f3 is empty. Wait — NWF1 is listed in move list as present? Wait — valid moves include "N: g8-f6", meaning my knight is on g8. But White’s knight is on f3? Looking at rank 3: a3 has PWF1, f3 is empty. But the encoding says NWF1 — where is it?\n\nWait — rechecking the board:\n\nRow 3: [PWF1,    ,    ,    ,    , NWF1,    ,    ] → So NWF1 is on f3.\n\nAh! Yes — White has a knight on f3. That\'s important.\n\nTherefore, B: c8-g4 would pin the knight on f3 to the king (if king is on e1 — yes, KWF0 on e1). So Bg4 pins the knight on f3 along the a8-h1 diagonal.\n\nThat is a very strong candidate.\n\nSo revised evaluation:\n\n- B: c8-g4 – Strong pin on the knight on f3, which is undefended. If White cannot easily break the pin, this could win material (e.g., if I later play ...e5, attacking f3, then Nxf3+?? loses the knight). Also increases pressure on the center.\n- P: d5-d4 – Strong central push, gains space, supports development, possibly kicking a knight or gaining control.\n- N: g8-f6 – Solid development, attacks e4, prepares to castle, supports central control.\n- P: e7-e6 – Prepares Bf8-b4 or Bd7, but passive.\n- P: e7-e5 – Immediate central challenge, but may create weaknesses.\n\nNow comparing Bg4 vs Nf6 vs d4.\n\nBg4 puts immediate pressure on White’s position. The knight on f3 is pinned against the king if the e-file is closed (which it is). White may have to play h3 or g3 to break the pin, losing tempo. Alternatively, if they ignore it, I can follow up with ...e5, attacking f3, and if they don\'t retreat, I win the knight.\n\nd5-d4 is also strong — it seizes space and activates my position. But after d5-d4, White can play exd4, and if I recapture with a piece, it opens the e-file. But I have no piece ready to recapture yet.\n\nMoreover, d5-d4 now might allow cxd4 later, opening the c-file for White’s rook.\n\nBut d4 is still good — it stakes a claim in the center.\n\nHowever, **B: c8-g4** is more forcing and disruptive. It immediately creates problems for White. They must address the pin.\n\nAdditionally, the pawn on a3 suggests White may be preparing queenside expansion or undermining c5. But that is slow.\n\nGiven that I have the chance to **pin and potentially win the knight on f3**, and that this move develops a piece to an active square, **B: c8-g4** is highly attractive.\n\nIs there any downside?  \n- I lose the ability to castle long? No — the bishop is moving from c8, which doesn\'t affect king safety on kingside. Castling kingside is still fine.\n- Can White play Nh4 to trade? After Bg4, White plays Nh4? But they don’t have a knight on h4 — only one knight on f3. They could play Ne5? But e5 is empty. Or h3? Bh5? But that’s slow.\n\nBest response for White would be h3 or Be3 or Qd3 to defend f3.\n\nBut I gain time and initiative.\n\nReason: B: c8-g4 immediately pressures White’s knight on f3, which is undefended and pinned to the king. This forces White into a reactive position, potentially losing time to break the pin or risking material loss. It’s a principled developing move that increases activity and creates tactical threats.\n\nAnswer: B: c8-g4'}
