ht from f3? But Nf3 is not present; knight is on f4)\n\nWait — White’s pieces near: Nb1, Bc1, Rf1, Kf1, Nf4. None of them can capture on e3 directly. So only options are:\n- Block: Nd2, Be3, Rf2, or Ke2\n- Or resign.\n\nTherefore, **Q: b6-e3** is **checkmate threat**, and unless White has a blocking piece ready, it's game over.\n\nIs b2 really underprotected? Let's verify:\n\n- Queen on b6 goes to e3 → now attacks b2 diagonally.\n- Is b2 attacked by any other piece? Currently, no.\n- Can White block? Only moves:\n  - Nd2: possible (Nb1-d2)\n  - Be3: bishop from c1 to e3\n  - Rf2: rook f1 to f2 (partially blocks, but not diagonal)\n  - Ke2: king moves out\n\nBut **Rf2 does NOT block the diagonal** — the queen on e3 attacks b2 along the diagonal (e3–b6), so only a piece on c1, d2, or e3 can block.\n\nSo only blockers are:\n- d2: Nd2 or Bd2 (no bishop on d2-side)\n- e3: Be3 or Ne3\n- Or Ke2\n\nSo yes — **Qe3 creates an unstoppable threat of Qxb2#** unless White plays Be3 or Nd2 immediately.\n\nBut crucially, **it's our move**, and we can play Qe3 **now**, and White cannot prevent it — so after our move, it's checkmate next move unless they block.\n\nThus, **Q: b6-e3** is a *zwischenzug* or direct threat that wins at least material or leads to mating net.\n\nReason: This move exploits the weak back rank and poor protection of b2. It creates an immediate and unavoidable threat of checkmate on b2, forcing White into a passive defense, likely losing material or leading to a decisive attack.\n\nAnswer: Q: b6-e3"}
