\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |BBF1|    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[B: a2-b1, P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, P: b3-b2, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, N: b8-a6, N: b8-d7, P: c6-c5, K: e8-d7, K: e8-d8, K: e8-e7, K: e8-f8, K: e8-g8, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, B: g1-f2, B: g1-e3, B: g1-d4, B: g1-c5, B: g1-b6, B: g1-h2, N: g4-e3, N: g4-e5, N: g4-f2, N: g4-f6, N: g4-h2, N: g4-h6, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6, R: h8-g8, R: h8-f8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: I am playing as Black, and the board is partially open with several pawns advanced. My king is still in the center (e8), but castling kingside (K: e8-g8) is a legal move, which would bring it to safety behind the h-file rook and g7-pawn. White has moved their king already (KWF1), so they cannot castle, and their king is exposed on f4. However, White has active pieces: two knights (on f2 and g4) and a dark-squared bishop on b2 targeting key central squares. My bishop on g1 is trapped and under threat — White’s knight on g4 can move to e5 or f6, potentially forking or threatening material. Additionally, my pawn on c6 is isolated and could become a target.\n\nEvaluation:\n- K: e8-g8 – Strong option. Castles kingside, tucking the king into safety while connecting the rooks. This is highly desirable given White\'s developed minor pieces.\n- N: g4-e3 – Captures a central pawn (PWF1 on d4? Wait — no, PWF1 is on a4). Actually, N:g4-e3 attacks nothing immediately but occupies e3, a strong central square. But it may be met by a capture if supported.\nWait — correction: N: g4 is a *White* knight! The move list shows N: g4-e3 etc. as valid moves for me — but that piece is white! This suggests a critical error.\n\nWait — rechecking: The move list says "N: g4-e3" is among *my* valid moves. But g4 has a **NBF1** — that’s a *Black* knight on g4? But in the board:\n\nRow 4: ... |    |    |    |    |    |KWF1|NBF1|NWF1| 4\n\nAh! Here it is:  \nf4: KWF1 (White King)  \ng4: NBF1 (Black Knight)  \nh4: NWF1 (White Knight)\n\nSo yes — I have a knight on g4! That’s excellent. It\'s centralized and active. But it\'s surrounded by enemy pieces — White king on f4, knight on h4. So moving it must be done carefully.\n\nNow evaluating again:\n- N: g4-e3 – Jumps to e3, attacking c2, d5, f5, etc. Possibly strong, but removes knight from g4 where it eyes h2 or f2.\n- N: g4-e5 – Central outpost, hard to dislodge, eyes f7 and d7. Strong candidate.\n- N: g4-f6 – Forks potential on g8 or e8? Not immediately, but pressures f6 near my own pawn structure. Also threatens discovered attacks.\n- N: g4-f2 – Attacks b1 (where a black bishop can go?) b1 is empty. f2 is defended by White king? f2 has BBF1 — wait no: a2 is BBF1? No:\n\nRow 2: a2: BBF1 (Black Bishop), rest empty except g1: BBF1? No:\n\nRow 1: g1: BBF1 — ah! I have a bishop on g1. But g1 is deep in White territory — that must be a typo?\n\nWait — let’s reconstruct the board properly.\n\nFrom the grid:\n\nRow 8: a8=RBF0, b8=NBF0, c8=empty, d8=empty, e8=KBF0, f8=empty, g8=empty, h8=RBF0  \nRow 7: a7=PBF0, b7=PBF0, c7=empty, d7=empty, e7=empty, f7=PBF0, g7=PBF0, h7=PBF0  \nRow 6: c6=PBF1  \nRow 4: a4=PWF1, f4=KWF1, g4=NBF1, h4=NWF1  \nRow 3: b3=PBF1, g3=PWF1  \nRow 2: a2=BBF1  \nRow 1: g1=BBF1  \n\nWait — g1 has BBF1 — that means a *Black* bishop on g1? But g1 is White’s corner — possible only if promoted or misplaced.\n\nBut earlier row 4 has a4 = PWF1 — White pawn on a4. Then b3 = PBF1 — Black pawn on b3 (passed?). Then a2 = BBF1 — Black bishop on a2? That makes sense — came from somewhere.\n\nAnd g1 = BBF1 — another Black bishop? That would mean I have two bishops — one on a2, one on g1.\n\nBut how did a Black bishop get to g1? Possibly via promotion or long maneuver.\n\nBut looking at the valid moves: B: g1-f2, B: g1-e3, etc. — so yes, I control a piece on g1 — a bishop.\n\nBut also a2 is a bishop — so two bishops? Yes, possible if one was promoted.\n\nBut then why is there a bishop on g1? And is it safe?\n\nWhite has king on f4 — very advanced, possibly vulnerable.\n\nMy knight on g4 is on a strong square — attacks e3, e5, f2, f6, h2, h6.\n\nBut f2 is adjacent to White’s king on f4 — not directly attacked yet.\n\nStrong options:\n- K: e8-g8 – Kingside castling. Very appealing. Gets king to safety, connects rooks, activates h8-rook. Since White king is stuck in center, this is good prophylaxis.\n- N: g4-e5 – Occupies a central outpost. From e5, the knight pressures f7, d7, c6, f3, g6 — but more importantly, it\'s stable and hard to remove. Also eyes the weakened light squares around White’s king.\n- N: g4-f6 – Checks? No, f6 is empty. But f6 is a strong square near White’s king. Could prepare pressure on f4 or set up tactics.\n- P: f7-f5 – Strikes in the center, challenging the diagonal. But weakens e6 and g6.\n- B: g1-h2 – Not listed, but B: g1-h2 isn\'t in valid moves — instead B: g1-f2, e3, d4, c5, b6, h2 are listed? Wait — B: g1-h2 is not listed, but B: g1-b6 is.\n\nWait — valid moves include: B: g1-f2, B: g1-e3, B: g1-d4, B: g1-c5, B: g1-b6, B: g1-h2 — oh, B: g1-h2 *is* listed.\n\nBut h2 is occupied? Row 2: only a2 has BBF1 — h2 is empty. So yes, bishop can go h2.\n\nB: g1-h2 attacks the White king from the diagonal! h2 is just one square away from f4 — along the diagonal h2-d6? No — h2 to f4 is not diagonal.\n\ng1 to h2 is a diagonal move? g1 to h2 is one square northeast — yes, diagonal. So BBF1 on g1 can go to h2.\n\nFrom h2, it eyes a9? No — but more importantly, it puts pressure toward the center and potentially supports an attack.\n\nBut better: from h2, it doesn’t directly attack f4 — that’s two files over.\n\nBut if combined with knight on g4, could create threats.\n\nHowever, the most urgent issue is king safety.\n\nI haven’t moved my king or rooks. The king is on e8, and I have castling rights (KBF0). There are no pieces between e8 and h8 — only f8 and g8 empty. So kingside castling is legal.\n\nWhite’s king is on f4 — very exposed. But I should not ignore my own vulnerability.\n\nBest plan: secure king first, then counterattack.\n\nCastling also activates the h8 rook.\n\nAnother idea: P: f7-f5 — opens lines, but too committal without king safety.\n\nN: g4-e5 — strong, but after castling, I can play that next.\n\nThe best immediate move is to castle.\n\nIs there any risk? Castling requires:\n- King not in check — e8 is not attacked\n- Doesn’t pass through check — f8 and g8 must not be attacked\n- No pieces between — clear\n\nWho attacks f8? White has knight on h4 (NWF1) — does it attack f8? h4 to f8 is 2 down, 2 left — not an L-shape. h4 to f5 is L-shape (2 down, 2 left). f8 is 4 ranks back — no.\n\nWhite bishop? They had a bishop on b2? No — in row 4, no bishop. Only knights and king.\n\nWhite’s pieces: \n- Pawns: a4, g3, h7? No — a4=PWF1, g3=PWF1 — that’s all?\n- Knights: h4 and ? Earlier: NWF1 on h4, and… any other?\n\nRow 4: h4=NWF1 — that’s one. Is there another? Valid moves include N: g4-e3 etc., but those are mine.\n\nWhite has only one knight? On h4.\n\nAnd king on f4.\n\nNo bishops visible for White.\n\nSo no one is attacking f8 or g8.\n\nThus, castling is safe.\n\nMoreover, after castling, I can follow up with N: g4-e5 or N: g4-f6, increasing pressure on the White king.\n\nAlternatively, N: g4-e3 might win material? e3 is empty. But is it defended? f4 is king — king defends e3? e3 is one diagonal away from f4 — yes, king on f4 controls e3.\n\nSo N: g4-e3 would be captured by king — NxK? No: N:e3, Kxf4? But f4 is occupied by king — so if knight goes to e3, king can take it only if adjacent.\n\ne3 and f4 are adjacent diagonally — yes. So Kf4 x Ne3 is possible.\n\nSo N: g4-e3 loses the knight for no gain.\n\nSimilarly, N: g4-f2 — f2 is not occupied, but is it safe? f2 is adjacent to f4 — king controls e2, e3, e4, f3, f5, g3, g4, g5. f2 is two down — not in range. So f2 is not controlled by king.\n\nBut is it attacked by anything else? No White pieces nearby. So N: g4-f2 is safe.\n\nBut what does it achieve? Attacks d1, e4, g4, h3 — not very useful.\n\nWhereas N: g4-e5 jumps to a strong central square, not under immediate attack, and exerts pressure.\n\ne5 is not attacked by White king — f4 to e5 is one diagonal — yes, adjacent! So Ke5 would be in check by king? No — same color. But the king on f4 *attacks* e5.\n\nSo if I play N: g4-e5, White king on f4 captures it — Kxe5.\n\nSo that loses the knight.\n\nTherefore, **any knight move to e3, e5, f2, f6, h2, h6 must avoid being captured**.\n\nCheck:\n\n- e3: adjacent to f4 → capturable\n- e5: adjacent to f4 → capturable\n- f2: f2 and f4 — same file, two apart — not adjacent → safe\n- f6: f6 and f4 — same file, two apart → not adjacent → safe\n- h2: h2 and f4 — far apart — not adjacent → safe\n- h6: h6 and f4 — different — not adjacent → safe\n\nSo only moves to f2, f6, h2, h6 are safe.\n\nNow evaluate safe ones:\n- N: g4-f2 – Lands on f2. From there, attacks d1, e4, g4, h3. d1 is empty, e4 is empty. Not very impactful. Might be rerouted later.\n- N: g4-f6 – f6 is strong. Pressures e8 (my king?), but more importantly, it\'s close to White’s king. From f6, next move could be f6-e4 or f6-g4, or even f6-e8+ if e8 vacated. But currently, it eyes the center. Also, if White plays g3-g4, I can consider f6-e4+, forking king and bishop? No bishop. But e4 is central. Strong post.\n- N: g4-h2 – h2 is corner. Not great for knight — limited mobility (only f1, g4, f3). Poor square.\n- N: g4-h6 – h6 is edge. Attacks f5, g4, f7. f5 is empty. Could support g7-g5 push or prepare rook lift.\n\nBest among knight moves: **N: g4-f6**\n\nIt places the knight on a