ve improved position.\n\n- After **P: f7-f5+**: Pf5+, king flees, f-file opens, king stuck in center, and now knight on g4 attacks f2, e3, h2 — very dangerous.\n\nAdditionally, after f5, the bishop on a2 may become stronger along the a2-g8 diagonal? a2 to g8 passes through g8 — but g8 has king.\n\nNot helpful.\n\nBut the **bishop on g1** — BBF1 on g1 — that’s a black bishop on g1.\n\ng1 is a dark square.\n\nFrom g1, it controls the long diagonal a8-h1 — which goes through e3, d4, c5, b6, a7.\n\nCurrently, that diagonal is open? Let’s see:\n\n- g1: BBF1 (black bishop)\n- f2: empty\n- e3: empty\n- d4: empty\n- c5: empty\n- b6: empty\n- a7: PBF0\n\nSo yes — **Bg1 controls e3, d4, c5, etc.**\n\nAfter **N: g4-e3**, the knight lands on e3 — which is on the bishop’s diagonal — so they support each other.\n\nBut after **P: f7-f5+**, the king is harassed, and then we can play Ne3+ next.\n\nBut why not deliver check **now** with the pawn?\n\nBecause **P: f7-f5+** forces the king to move, and then we can follow up with more attacks.\n\nBut **N: g4-e3+** also forces king to move — and it’s a piece sacrifice only if the knight is lost, but it’s not — it’s a check, not a capture.\n\nNe3+ is not a capture — e3 is empty.\n\nSo it’s a discovered-style check via knight move.\n\nBut **both are checks**.\n\nNow, which is stronger?\n\nLet’s simulate:\n\nOption 1: **N: g4-e3+**\n- Ne3+\n- King must move: Ke4-d5, Ke4-f5 (invalid), Ke4-e5, Ke4-d4, Ke4-f4\n- Best: Ke4-d5 or Ke4-f4\n- Suppose Ke4-d5\n- Then Black can play f7-f5 next, attacking d5? f5 attacks e4, not d5.\n- Or Rf8-e8+, winning material?\n\nBut White may play Nb1-c3 or something.\n\nBut knight on e3 is now very strong — attacks d5, f5, c4, etc.\n\nOption 2: **P: f7-f5+**\n- Pf5+\n- Ke4-d5 (only safe square? e5 is attacked by bishop on g1? Bg1 attacks e3, d4, c5 — not e5)\n- e5 is light square — not on diagonal\n- So Ke4-e5 possible?\n- But e5: is it attacked?\n- Bishop on g1: dark squares — e5 is light → not attacked\n- Knight