entral.\n\nHowever, is d4 defended? Currently no.\n\nBut White has no immediate way to kick it.\n\nBut **best move** may be **B: g1-c5** — c5 is dark, and it attacks b4 and d4, and eyes the weak d4 and e3 squares.\n\nBut c5 is well placed.\n\nAlternatively, **B: g1-b6** — targets the a7-g1 diagonal, possibly pressuring a7 or supporting ...a5.\n\nBut less effective.\n\nNow, another idea: **P: a7-a5** — prepares ...a5-a4, gaining space, preventing Nb1-c3.\n\nBut slow.\n\nBest developing move is **B: g1-d4** — occupies the center immediately.\n\nBut risk: White may play c3 or b3 to attack it.\n\nBut Black can support with ...Nc6 or ...e5.\n\nBut currently no such support.\n\nAlternatively, **N: b8-d7** is solid.\n\nBut given that the bishop has only one good diagonal, and d4 or e3 are strong, **B: g1-e3** is excellent — e3 is central, supports f4 or d4, and connects with kingside.\n\ne3 is on the same diagonal as g1: g1→f2→e3 — yes.\n\nAnd e3 is a strong square.\n\nAlso, it eyes f4 and d4.\n\nBut d4 is better.\n\nWait — what about **B: g1-f2**? f2 is dark, but less central.\n\nNo.\n\n**B: g1-d4** seems strongest.\n\nBut let’s reconsider the pawn move: **P: b3-b2**\n\nAlthough it doesn’t promote immediately, it forces White to deal with the pawn.\n\nIf Black plays b3-b2, then White must consider: if they don’t move the knight from b1, then next move b2-b1=Q is impossible — but Black can play b2xc1=Q or b2-b1=N (knight fork?) — but b1 is occupied.\n\nSo Black cannot move to b1 while knight is there.\n\nSo the only way to promote is if the knight moves away.\n\nSo the threat is only if White voluntarily moves the knight.\n\nOtherwise, the pawn is blocked.\n\nSo not a strong threat.\n\nUnless Black can force the knight to move.\n\nBut no.\n\nSo better to develop.\n\nNow, another move: **P: g5-g4** — gains space, prepares h7-h5-h4 or g4-g3.\n\nBut weakens h4.\n\nNot urgent.\n\nBest is to develop the bishop.\n\nBetween B: g1-c5 and B: g1-d4, which is better?\n\nd4 is more ce