ressure. The fact that it doesn’t give check isn’t fatal — it’s still strong.\n\nWait — is the pawn on b2 defended? Looking at the board:\n\n- White has pawns on a4 (PWF1), b2? No — b2 is occupied by a black bishop? No, current state shows:\n\nRow 2: |BBF1|    |    |    |    |    |    |    | → So b2 is empty.\n\nWait! a1 is BBF1 (Black Bishop), and valid move is **B: a1-b2** — so b2 is currently empty. Then why is it listed as capturing? It\'s not a capture — it\'s just a move.\n\nBut the move list says "B: a1-b2", not "B: a1xb2", so likely non-capture.\n\nBut then what\'s on b2? Empty. So it\'s a quiet development.\n\nBut earlier I thought there was a pawn — no, white pawn on a4, not on b2.\n\nLooking again:\n\n- Row 2: a2: BBF1 (Black Bishop), b2: empty\n\n- Row 1: a1: BBF1 (another Black Bishop?), b1: NWF1 (White Knight)\n\nWait — two black bishops on a1 and a2? That can\'t be.\n\nLet’s re-read the board:\n\nRow 8: a8: RBF0, b8: NBF0, ..., g8: KBF1, h8: empty  \nRow 7: a7: PBF0, b7: PBF0, f7: PBF0, h7: PBF0  \nRow 6: c6: PBF1  \nRow 5: g5: PBF1  \nRow 4: a4: PWF1, c4: KWF1, g4: PWF1  \nRow 3: b3: PBF1  \nRow 2: a2: BBF1  \nRow 1: a1: BBF1, b1: NWF1\n\na1 and a2 both have black bishops? That would mean two bishops on dark squares, but a1 is dark, a2 is light — so different colors? But both labeled BBF1 — Black Bishop.\n\nBut a1 is dark square, a2 is light square — so possible, but how did both get there?\n\nMore importantly, the move **B: a1-b2** — from a1 (black bishop) to b2 (empty). b2 is a light square. a1 to b2 is diagonal — yes, one square northeast.\n\nThis move develops the bishop to b2, where it controls the long diagonal (c3, d4, e5, f6, g7, h8). Importantly, **g7 and h8 are sensitive squares near White’s king** — but White’s king is on c4, not near h8.\n\nBut **e5, f6, g7** — and Black has a pawn on g5 (PBF1)? No, g5 is empty. Black has pawns on c6, g5? Row 5: g5: PBF1 — yes, pawn on g5.\n\nAfter Bb2, the bishop eyes the diagonal toward h8, but more imp