ward means toward lower ranks — down the board.\n\nSo Black pawns move from rank 7→6→5→4→3→2→1.\n\nFrom b3 (rank 3) to b2 (rank 2) — that’s forward one square.\n\nYes — so P: b3-b2 is a legal pawn move.\n\nBut b2 is empty — so it\'s just advancing a pawn.\n\nBut a2 has BBF1 — Black Bishop on a2.\n\nSo Black has a bishop on a2 — which is odd — likely developed via Bc8-a2 or something, but now it\'s trapped? Because after b3-b2, the pawn advances, but doesn\'t affect the bishop.\n\nBut more importantly: look at the white king: KWF1 on b4? No — wait:\n\nRow 4: |    |KWF1|    |    |    |    |PWF1|    | 4\n\nSo b4: KWF1 — White King on b4!\n\nThat’s extremely exposed! The white king is on b4 — far from safety, in the center, on an open file.\n\nThis is critical.\n\nBlack has a bishop on a2 — which is on the long diagonal a2-g8. From a2, the bishop is pointing directly at the white king on b4!\n\nDiagonal: a2 → b3 → c4 → d5 → e6 → f7 → g8\n\nKing is on b4 — which is adjacent to b3.\n\nBut b3 has a black pawn.\n\nThe diagonal from a2 to g8 passes through: a2, b3, c4, d5, e6, f7, g8.\n\nThe white king is on b4 — not on that diagonal.\n\nIs the bishop attacking the king? No — b4 is not on the same diagonal as a2 unless it\'s a2-b3-c4, but b4 is one square right.\n\na2 to b4 is two up and one right — not diagonal.\n\na2 is light square? a1 is dark (standard chess), so a2 is light.\n\nb4: b-file, rank 4 — b1 is light, so b4 is dark.\n\nSo a2 (light) to b4 (dark) — not same color — so not on same diagonal.\n\nSo the bishop does not attack b4.\n\nBut what about checks?\n\nCan we exploit the exposed king?\n\nLet’s consider tactical shots.\n\nNow, P: b3-b2 — advances the pawn from b3 to b2. After this, the black pawn is on b2.\n\nStill, the bishop on a2 is now behind the pawn — blocked.\n\nBut b2 is adjacent to the white king on b4? No — b2 and b4 are two squares apart vertically.\n\nBut c3 is between.\n\nWait — is there a discovered attack?\n\nNot obvious.\n\nBack to evaluation.\