knight on f1 and threatens material gain. Strong tactical shot.\n- Qe4-b1: Also attacks f1 and adds pressure, but less centralized than c2.\n- Qe4-g2: Threatens checkmate on h1 (Qg2xh1#) if not stopped — this is very strong!\n    - After Qg2xh1#, White must respond immediately to avoid losing.\n    - To stop this, White must capture or block — only options are Ng3 (knight blocks) or Ke2 (king flees).\n    - But Ng3? Qxh1 anyway, winning the rook.\n    - Ke2? Then Qxh1 still wins full rook, and the white king is exposed.\n- Pawns like b7-b5 or f7-f5 aim to push forward, but they don’t address immediate tactical threats or opportunities.\n\nReason: The most forcing and strongest move is Qe4-g2, as it creates an immediate, unavoidable threat of checkmate on h1 (Qg2xh1#). White cannot ignore this. The only way to prevent mate is by moving the king (Ke2) or blocking with Ng3. Either way, Black captures on h1 with the queen, winning a full rook for essentially free, while also opening lines against the exposed white king. This move maximizes pressure and gains significant material or leads to a decisive attack.\n\nAnswer: Q: e4-g2'}
