PBF1), d4 is empty.\n\nSo White could potentially play something like ... move a piece to d4 to block, but on White’s turn — but right now, **it\'s Black to move**, and after Qe4+, White must respond.\n\nBut the point is: **Q: g2-e4+ is check**, and likely very strong, possibly leading to winning material or even mate.\n\nOther moves like P: b7-b5 or N: b8-c6 are slow in comparison.\n\nAlso, **Q: g2-d2** is on the same rank (rank 2), but king is on b4 (rank 4), so no check.\n\nBut **e4 is rank 4**, same as king.\n\nThus, **Q: g2-e4** is the strongest move.\n\nIt\'s in the valid move list: yes — "Q: g2-e4" is listed.\n\nReason: It delivers a strong check with the queen to e4, attacking the exposed white king on b4 along the 4th rank. The king has limited mobility, and no White piece can immediately capture or effectively block without losing material. This increases pressure dramatically and may lead to tactical wins such as capturing the king or forcing fatal concessions.\n\nAnswer: Q: g2-e4'}
