\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |NWF1| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[B: a2-b1, B: a2-b3, P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, R: b5-a5, R: b5-b4, R: b5-b3, R: b5-b2, R: b5-b1, R: b5-b6, R: b5-b7, R: b5-b8, R: b5-c5, R: b5-d5, R: b5-e5, R: b5-f5, P: c4-c3, N: f7-d6, N: f7-d8, N: f7-e5, N: f7-h6, N: f7-h8, P: g4-g3, K: g8-f8, K: g8-g7, K: g8-h8, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and my king is on g8 (KBF1), currently safe but somewhat exposed in the endgame. White’s king is on d4 (KWF1), quite advanced and active. White has a knight on h1 (NWF1) and a bishop on a2 (BBF1), both undeveloped or awkwardly placed. My material includes a rook on b5 (RBF1), knight on f7 (NBF1), pawns on a7, c4, g4, h7, and a potential passed pawn on the a-file if advanced. The board is mostly open, and there are limited pieces left, suggesting an endgame with tactical possibilities.\n\nWhite’s king is centralized and could become dangerous, especially since my own king is still on the edge. However, my rook on b5 is well-placed to control the 5th rank and potentially pressure White’s king or pawns. The pawn on c4 is isolated but could be supported. My knight on f7 has several options, including attacking key central squares. The pawn on a7 can advance two squares (a7-a5) to support a potential queenside push.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **R: b5-a5**: Attacks the a-pawn, which could be useful if White tries to promote, but doesn’t increase pressure significantly.\n- **R: b5-b1**: Targets the weak back rank and threatens the white king, especially since White has no rook or queen to defend it. This could force the king to move and lose tempo.\n- **R: b5-b7**: Not valid — not in the list, so skipped.\n- **N: f7-d6** or **N: f7-e5**: Develops the knight toward the center, eyeing potential forks or attacks on d4 (the white king). N-e5 would put the knight very close to the king.\n- **P: a7-a5**: Pushes the pawn, possibly preparing ...a5-a4 and further advancement, but may allow White counterplay.\n- **K: g8-h8**: Safe but passive; not urgent.\n- **R: b5-f5** or **R: b5-e5**: Could cut off the white king’s escape or support an attack.\n\nMost promising:\n- **R: b5-b1** stands out because it delivers a discovered threat: the white king is on d4, and moving the rook to b1 puts the king in direct check from the rook along the first rank. Since White has no immediate piece to interpose or capture the rook (only a knight on h1 and bishop on a2), this is likely **check**, and possibly even **checkmate** depending on whether the king can escape.\n\nLet’s verify:  \nAfter **R: b5-b1**, the black rook moves to b1. The white king is on d4. Is it in check?  \nYes — rook on b1 controls the entire 1st rank. King on d4 is on d1? No — king is on d4, which is rank 4, not rank 1. So **no check**. My mistake.\n\nSo R:b5-b1 is not check. It threatens the back rank, but White can easily move the king away later. Still, it increases pressure.\n\nBetter idea: **N: f7-e5**  \nThis places the knight on e5, attacking d3, f3, c6, g6, and importantly, **can fork the king and other pieces**. But more relevant: from e5, it eyes d3 and f3, close to d4.\n\nEven better: **R: b5-e5** — cuts off the king’s retreat and controls the 5th rank. But not as forcing.\n\nWait — what about **N: f7-d6**? That attacks e4, c4, b5, b7, e8, f5 — not directly impactful.\n\nAlternatively, **R: b5-b7** — not allowed in move list.\n\nNow, consider **R: b5-b1** again — it threatens the a1 corner and future mate ideas, especially since White has a knight on h1 that might get trapped. But still slow.\n\nAnother strong candidate: **N: f7-e5** — centralizes the knight, prepares to jump to d3 or f3, potentially forking king and rook/bishop.\n\nBut look at **R: b5-f5** — controls the f-file, and if the king moves, could restrict it.\n\nWait — **P: c4-c3**? This advances the pawn one step, threatening promotion. But it’s only on c4, so c3 is just one step forward — not immediately dangerous.\n\nBut after c4-c3, next move c3-c2 and then c2-c1=Q could be powerful, especially if supported by rook or knight.\n\nBut White can respond to c3 with Kxc3.\n\nHowever, if we play **R: b5-b1**, then after Kb3 (if played), we can play c4-c3+, forcing the king to move, and continue with strong pressure.\n\nBut perhaps the most forcing and strongest move is **R: b5-b1** — it activates the rook to the only open file, threatens infiltration, and coordinates with the knight and king.\n\nBut let's reconsider: **N: f7-e5**\n\nFrom e5, the knight attacks d3, f3, c6, g6, and crucially, **puts pressure near the white king**. If White ever plays Ke3 or Kd3, Nf3+ could win material.\n\nAlso, it prepares to reroute to d3 if possible.\n\nAlternatively, **R: b5-e5** — same rank, but blocks the knight.\n\nBut **R: b5-e5** is in the list: yes, R: b5-e5 is allowed (as part of R: b5-[file]5).\n\nBut **R: b5-b1** is more flexible.\n\nWait — another idea: **P: a7-a5** — starts a pawn storm on the queenside. But slow.\n\nBest practical choice: **R: b5-b1** — it doubles down on the b-file and first rank, keeps options open, and increases activity.\n\nBut let's see: is there a tactic?\n\nWhite’s king on d4 is vulnerable to checks.\n\nMy knight on f7 to d6 doesn't help.\n\nWhat if I play **N: f7-e5**, then next move Ne5-d3+? That would be a fork if d3 is not defended.\n\nCurrently, d3 is not attacked. White has no pieces defending d3.\n\nAfter Ne5-d3+, the knight checks the king on d4. The king must move: to c3, e3, c5, e5, etc. But if it moves, I can