\nSo if Black plays N: e5-f7, the knight walks into a pinned square? Not necessarily pinned, but **after** ...Nf7, White can play Bxf7+, winning material with check — which would be disastrous for Black.\n\nSo N: e5-f7?? is a blunder due to Bxf7+.\n\nSimilarly, N: e5-g6 — possible, but less effective.\n\nBest move is **N: e5-d3+**, delivering check and attacking the center.\n\nIs there any other check? K: g8-f7+? No — f7 is not attacking d4. Only knight moves can deliver check here.\n\nSo N: e5-d3 is not only good — it’s likely the best move on the board.\n\nReason: It delivers a check to the exposed white king on d4, gains tempo, activates a piece, and potentially disrupts White’s coordination. The knight move to d3 increases pressure on the center and may lead to winning material if White’s king is forced into a worse position.\n\nAnswer: N: e5-d3'}
