ks the e4 square**, where the white king stands. So bishop on b3 would pin or at least pressure the king if there’s a piece in between — currently, e4 has only the king. So it’s a direct attack: Bb3 checks the white king!\n\nYes! King on e4, bishop on b3: same diagonal? b3 to e4 — that’s b3-c4-d5-e6? No — that’s not aligned. Let\'s see: b3 is b-file, rank 3; e4 is e-file, rank 4. Difference: +3 files, +1 rank. That’s not a diagonal. Diagonals require equal change in file and rank. Here, Δfile=3, Δrank=1 — not equal. So not diagonal.\n\nWhat diagonal is b3 on? b3-c4-d5-e6-f7-g8 — that’s one. Or b3-a4 (backward). So b3 does not attack e4.\n\nWhere would attack e4? A bishop on d6, c5, f7, or a4, etc. So a2-b3 doesn’t help.\n\nAlternative: what about bishop to d5? But not in options.\n\nOnly two bishop moves: b1 or b3.\n\na2-b1: b1 is even worse — c2, d3, e4? b1 to e4: Δfile=3, Δrank=3 — yes! b1-c2-d3-e4. That’s a diagonal. So **bishop on b1 attacks e4 directly** — and would deliver check!\n\nYes! So B: a2-b1 is a discovered check possibility? No — moving the bishop from a2 to b1: let\'s see path.\n\na2 is a-file, rank 2; b1 is b-file, rank 1. So from a2 to b1 is a diagonal move (a2-b1), which is one square diagonally down-right. And from b1, the diagonal continues: b1-c2-d3-e4. So yes, if the bishop moves to b1, it will be pointing directly at the white king on e4, and since no pieces are in between (c2, d3 are empty), **B: a2-b1 delivers check**.\n\nThat’s powerful.\n\nIs it safe? After Bb1+, White must respond: move king, block, or capture.\n\nCan White capture the bishop? Only if a piece can take on b1. Currently, no white piece attacks b1. King is on e4, too far. Knight on f5 — can it go to c2 or d3? No, not immediately. So no capture.\n\nCan White block? Possible future with Nd3, but not immediately — no piece can interpose now.\n\nSo king must move. From e4, king can go to d4, d5, e5, f4, f5 — but f5 has knight. So available: d4, d5, e5, f4.\n\nBut this forces the white king to move, gains tempo, and likely drives it back.\n\nMoreover, after such a check, I gain initiative.\n\nCompare to other moves:\n\n- B: a2-b1+ is an immediate check, strong.\n- Other moves are quiet.\n\nAlso, consider: after Bb1+, if White plays Ke5 or Kd5, I can consider ...g4-g3? Still not helpful.\n\nOr perhaps follow up with king centralization.\n\nBut B: a2-b1 seems strongest.\n\nWait — is b1 occupied? Current board: b1 is empty (from the grid: row 1 is all empty except possibly corners — a1, h1 not mentioned, but from layout, a1 is empty, b1 is empty). Yes, b1 is free.\n\nSo B: a2-b1 is legal and delivers check.\n\nWhy is it not listed as "check" in the move notation? Doesn\'t matter — the move is valid.\n\nNow, among the valid moves, B: a2-b1 is best.\n\nOther considerations: P: a7-a5 — gains space, but slow.\n\nK: g8-f7 — decent, but less urgent than delivering check.\n\nThe check disrupts White\'s coordination and may win material or lead to king exposure.\n\nReason: B: a2-b1 delivers a check, forcing the white king to move immediately, gaining tempo and initiative. It activates a minor piece to a strong diagonal, and exploits the exposed position of the white king on e4. No other move provides such immediate pressure.\n\nAnswer: B: a2-b1'}
