\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, P: c4-c3, P: g4-g3, B: g6-f5, B: g6-e4, B: g6-d3, B: g6-c2, B: g6-b1, B: g6-h5, B: g6-f7, B: g6-e8, K: g7-f6, K: g7-f7, K: g7-f8, K: g7-g8, K: g7-h6, K: g7-h8, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and my king is on g7, surrounded by limited mobility. The white king is on d2, quite distant, but not immediately threatening. My pieces include two pawns (a7, h7), a bishop on g6, and pawns on c4, g4, and g5. White has no visible material except the king. My position is somewhat passive, but I have active piece potential. Notably, the black bishop on g6 has several diagonals open and could become a strong attacking or supporting piece. My king is near the edge, so safety is a concern, but there’s no immediate check.\n\nThe most urgent considerations are development, central control, and king safety. Advancing pawns can help gain space or promote, while the bishop can exert influence over key diagonals. Castling is not possible due to king movement (KBF1). En passant is not currently relevant since no pawn just moved two squares in the last turn (from White's side).\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: a7-a6 – Quiet move, prepares a7-a5 later but doesn’t challenge anything now.\n- P: a7-a5 – Commits the pawn early; could be useful for future queenside play, but slow.\n- P: c4-c3 – Pushes forward, restricts White’s options if a piece were to develop to d2, but currently minimal impact.\n- P: g4-g3 – Weakens the kingside pawn structure and may create holes; little benefit.\n- B: g6-f5 – Develops the bishop to an active diagonal, eyes weak squares like e4, d3, and potentially threatens the center.\n- B: g6-e4 – Very strong; centralizes the bishop, controls many key squares, supports potential king motion or future attacks.\n- B: g6-d3 – Ambitious, but possibly premature; long diagonal but may be chased.\n- B: g6-c2 – Overextended; likely gets trapped or traded without compensation.\n- B: g6-b1 – Too passive, retreats from action.\n- B: g6-h5 – Sideways move, gains nothing.\n- B: g6-f7 – Moves into own pawn chain; blocks the g7 king’s escape and loses tempo.\n- B: g6-e8 – Retreats backwards, poor.\n- K: g7-f6 – Centralizing the king, very promising in endgames; improves king activity.\n- K: g7-f7 – Retreats slightly, less optimal than f6.\n- K: g7-f8 – Retreats further, passive.\n- K: g7-g8 – Stays in place, no progress.\n- K: g7-h6 – Kingside edge, limited use.\n- K: g7-h8 – Cornering the king, bad.\n- P: h7-h6 – Prevents any theoretical Ng5 ideas, but creates a weakness on g6 and limits the bishop.\n- P: h7-h5 – Gains space, opens the h-file slightly, and maintains flexibility.\n\nBest moves appear to be:\n- B: g6-e4 – Strong central control, activates the bishop.\n- K: g7-f6 – Activates the king, crucial in endgames.\n- P: a7-a5 – Gains space on the queenside.\n\nReason: In endgame-like situations (few pieces), king activity is paramount. However, improving the worst-placed piece (the bishop) also makes sense. B: g6-e4 places the bishop on one of the most powerful central squares, controlling both light and dark squares, influencing d5, f5, d3, f3, and c2. It becomes extremely hard to dislodge and supports any future plan. Meanwhile, the king will eventually go to f6, but moving it now might allow White counterplay if they activate their king. Since White’s king is far away, we can afford to improve our bishop first.\n\nAnswer: B: g6-e4"}
