romotion. B: g6-f5 or B: g6-h5 helps control central/diagonal squares, but less urgent.\n- **K: g7-f6 / f7 / f8 / g8 / h6 / h7 / h8** – King movement. K: g7-f6 stands out as it centralizes the king and brings it closer to support both the g4 and h4 pawns. It also prepares to escort a pawn to promotion. K: g7-f7 is backward. K: g7-h8 or h7 is lateral without clear benefit.\n\nBest candidate: **K: g7-f6** – Centralizes the king, activates it toward the center, and positions it to support both the g4 and h4 pawns' advance. From f6, the king can go to f5 or g5 to shield advancing pawns or even push them to promotion. This is a critical step in converting material advantage into a win.\n\nReason: The key to winning this endgame is promoting a pawn. The king must be active. Moving the king from g7 to f6 improves its activity significantly and is more impactful than minor pawn pushes or bishop shuffles. The bishop already controls useful diagonals, so prioritizing king centralization maximizes winning chances.\n\nAnswer: K: g7-f6"}
