e or material gain. B-e4-d3 attacks d3, potentially preparing for a net against the king.\n- **B: e4-f5 / e4-g6 / e4-h7**: Less useful; they move away from the center of action.\n- **Q: f3-e2**: Threatens check! Qe2+ would fork or check the White king on d2. This is very strong.\n- **Q: f3-d3 / f3-c3 / f3-b3 / f3-a3**: All increase pressure on the d2 king, cutting escape routes.\n- **Q: f3-f2**: Delivers check: Qf2+! King must move, possibly leading to loss of control.\n- **K: f6 moves**: King is safe for now; moving it isn’t urgent.\n- **P: g4-g3**: Pushes the pawn toward g2-g1 promotion, but g3 doesn’t immediately threaten promotion.\n\nCritical line: **Q: f3-e2** gives check and attacks the White king directly. After Qe2+, White must play Kd1 or Kc1 (if allowed), but then Black can follow up with B-e4-c2, forking or trapping the king, or advancing pawns.\n\nEven stronger: **Q: f3-f2** delivers check (Qf2+), forcing Kc1 (only legal move, assuming Kd1 is attacked). Then Black can play Qf1# next move if not stopped — but wait: after Qf3-f2+, Kd1 is actually possible unless under attack.\n\nWait — current White king is on d2. If Black plays **Q: f3-f2**, that is Qf2+, attacking d2 king. The king can go to d1 or e1.\n\nBut better: **Q: f3-e2+**! This checks the king directly. After Kd1, Black plays **B: e4-c2**, attacking b1 and controlling c1 and d1 — this traps the king: next move Qe1# or Qxc2.\n\nAlternatively, **Q: f3-e2+**, Kb1? But king cannot go to b1 from d2 unless c1/d1 blocked. Only legal king moves from d2 are c1, c2, c3, d1, d3, e1, e2, e3. So after Qe2+, king must go to c1, d1, e1, e2, or e3.\n\nBut **Q: f3-e2+** is check. Suppose Kd1. Then **B: e4-c2** attacks b1 and a3, and controls c2–b1 diagonal. Now White has no good moves — any king move allows Qe1#. For example:\n- Kd1 → Bc2+ (discovered check?) No, not discovered. Wait: after Qe2+ Kd1, then Bc2 blocks escape and prepares Qe1#.\n\nActually, after **Qe2+ Kd1**, **Qe1#** is mate — because queen moves from e2 to e1, checking the king on d1 with support from bishop on e4 (which controls c2, but not e1). Does the queen on e1 deliver check? Yes. Is there any piece blocking? No. Can king escape? From d1, adjacent squares: c1, c2, d2, e1, e2. e1 is occupied by queen. d2 is attacked by queen? Yes, but not necessarily occupied. But after Qe1+, king must leave d1, but c1, c2, e1, e2 — e1 is attacked, d2 is attacked by queen on e2 before, but now queen is on e1.\n\nWait: sequence:\n1. Qf3-e2+: checks king on d2\n   - King moves: Kc1, Kd1, Ke1, Ke2, Ke3, Kc2, Kc3, Kd3 — all possible unless blocked.\n\nSuppose **Qf3-e2+**, then **Kd1**.\nNow, **Qe2-e1#** — yes! Queen moves down one square to e1, delivering check. The king on d1 cannot capture (no protection), and cannot go to c1 (attacked by queen on e1? Diagonal a5-e1? No — e1 to c1 is two left, not diagonal. Queen attacks along rank, file, diagonal.\n\nQueen on e1 attacks:\n- Entire 1st rank: a1 to h1\n- Entire e-file: e2 to e8\n- Diagonals: d2, c3, b4, a5 and f2, g3, h4\n\nSo from e1, queen attacks d1? No — d1 is one left, not diagonal. Diagonal from e1 is f2, g3… or d2, c3…\n\nSo **queen on e1 does NOT attack d1** — same rank? No, e1 and d1 are adjacent on rank 1, but queen on e1 attacks entire rank 1 — YES! Rank 1: all files.\n\nYes! Queen on e1 attacks all of rank 1 — including a1, b1, c1, d1, e1, f1, etc.\n\nSo **Qe1+** attacks d1 — yes, same rank.\n\nTherefore: **Qf3-e2+**, forces Kd1 (or Kc1, etc.), then **Qe2-e1#** is checkmate — because queen moves to e1, controls entire rank 1, so king on d1 is in check and cannot go to c1 (also on rank 1, attacked), c2 (not attacked yet?), e1 (occupied), e2 (attacked by queen on e1? e2 is on e-file — yes, queen on e1 controls e2.\n\nAfter Qe1+, king on d1:\n- c1: attacked by queen (a1-h1)\n- c2: not attacked by any black piece currently\n- d2: not attacked\n- e1: occupied\n- e2: attacked by queen\n\nSo king can go to c2 — **not mated**.\n\nOh! So **Qe2-e1+** is not mate — king