 a1: attacked by queen on b2? Yes — b2 controls a1 diagonally.\n        - a3: attacked by pawn on a4? Yes — a4 pawn controls a3.\n        - b1: attacked by queen? Yes — b2 queen attacks b1 vertically.\n        - b3: not occupied, but is it attacked? Queen on b2 attacks b3 vertically.\n        - So all surrounding squares are covered → this is checkmate.\n\n- Other queen moves like c3-a1 or c3-a3 also attack the king, but:\n  - Q: c3-a1 would be check, but king can go to b1 or b2 — not mate.\n  - Q: c3-a3+ → king can go to b1 or b2.\n\nOnly Q: c3-b2 delivers immediate checkmate.\n\n- Bishop moves don't lead to immediate mate; they waste time.\n- King moves are slow and unnecessary when mate is available.\n- Pawn moves (g4-g3 or a4-a3) are irrelevant now.\n\nReason: Q: c3-b2 is checkmate. It attacks the white king on a2 and covers all escape squares (a1, a3, b1, b3), with a4 pawn covering a3 and queen covering the rest. This ends the game immediately.\n\nAnswer: Q: c3-b2"}
