nt, **has moved** → correct notation.\nBut the label is KWF1 — so F1 means “flag for 2-step = F (no), moved = 1 (yes)” → so white king has moved.\n\nSimilarly, KBF1: Black King, has moved.\n\nNow, Black to move.\n\nBlack material: \n- King (f6)\n- Bishop (e4)\n- Pawns: a4, g4, f5\n\nWhite material:\n- King (f2)\n\nThat’s it? Only kings and a few pawns?\n\nYes — rest of board is empty.\n\nSo this is an endgame with minimal material.\n\nCurrent position:\n- Black king on f6\n- Black bishop on e4\n- Black pawns on a4, g4, f5\n- White king on f2\n\nWait — pawn on f5 is labeled PBF1 — Black pawn on f5.\n\nSo pawns:\n- a4: Black\n- e4: Black Bishop\n- f5: Black pawn\n- g4: Black pawn\n\nAll Black pawns — no White pawns.\n\nWhite has only king on f2.\n\nSo Black has a significant material advantage: bishop + three pawns vs nothing.\n\nGoal: promote one of the pawns, especially since bishop alone can\'t checkmate, but with a queen it’s easy.\n\nLet’s analyze key elements:\n\n- Black to move.\n- White king is on f2 — central-ish, but low.\n- Black king is on f6 — quite advanced.\n- Black has pawns on a4, f5, g4 — all potentially capable of promotion.\n    - a4: needs 4 moves to reach a1 (a4→a3→a2→a1)\n    - f5: f5→f4→f3→f2? But f2 is occupied by White king.\n    - g4: g4→g3→g2→g1\n\nBut note: after g4→g3 (one of the valid moves), then next move g3→g2, etc.\n\nHowever, the **bishop on e4** is very active — controls diagonal toward a8-h1.\n\nIt can target the promotion squares, especially if White tries to block.\n\nBut most importantly: **Black can start advancing pawns safely**.\n\nLet’s evaluate promising moves:\n\n1. **P: g4-g3** — advances g-pawn. This is a step toward promoting. After g3, next g2, then queening. White king may try to stop it, but it’s far away (f2). f2 to g1 is two moves (f2-e3-d4-c5-b6-a7? No — wrong direction). From f2 to g1 is just f2-g1 in two moves: f2-g3, then g3-g1? No — g3 to g1 is two steps down — not allowed. f2 → f1 → g1? Or f2 → g3 → h2 → g1? Too slow.\n\nActually, distance from White king (f2) to g1 is: f2 → g3 → h4 → g5… no, that’s away. To reach g1 to stop g-pawn, path is f2 → g3 → h2 → g1? Not efficient.\n\nBetter: f2 → g3 → f4 → g5 → h6 → g7 → h8? Not helpful.\n\nTo stop g-pawn: once Black plays g4-g3, then g3-g2, then g2-g1=Q.\n\nFrom f2, White king would need to go f2 → e3 → d4 → c5 → b6 → a7 to stop a-pawn, or f2 → g3 → f4 → g5 → h6 to stop h-pawn — but g-pawn is closer.\n\nBut f2 to g1: shortest path is f2 → g3 → h2 → g1? No, h2 is not on the way.\n\nf2 → g1 is only one diagonal move — **f2 to g1 is adjacent diagonally** — yes! f2 and g1 are diagonally adjacent.\n\nSo if Black plays g2-g1=?, White king on f2 can capture on g1 if it gets there.\n\nBut currently g-pawn is on g4.\n\nAfter g4-g3, then g3-g2, then g2-g1.\n\nAt g2, the pawn is on g2, White king on f2 — then **f2 can capture on g1 after g2-g1**, because when pawn promotes, it becomes queen on g1, and king on f2 can capture it.\n\nBut during g2-g1=Q, the square g1 becomes occupied — king on f2 is adjacent — so after promotion, White can play Kf2xg1.\n\nSo promotion on g1 is not safe — king guards g1.\n\nSame for a1: a1 is far — but who guards a1? Only if king comes.\n\nBut a-pawn: a4-a3 — then a3-a2 — a2-a1.\n\nDistance from f2 to a1: 5 files and 1 rank — very far. f2 to a1 takes at least 6 moves (e.g., f2-e3-d4-c5-b6-a7-a1? No. Better: f2-e3-d4-c5-b5-a4-a3-a2-a1 — too long.\n\nManhattan distance: |f-a|=5, |2-1|=1 → max(5,1)=5 moves minimum.\n\nWhereas a-pawn from a4 to a1: 3 moves: a4-a3, a3-a2, a2-a1.\n\nSo Black can promote the a-pawn before White king can reach a1.\n\nSimilarly, f5-pawn: f5-f4-f3-f2 — but f2 is occupied by White king.\n\nSo f5-f4 is not in valid moves? Wait — valid moves include:\n\n[P: a4-a3, ... P: g4-g3]\n\nBut no f5-f4? Why?\n\nBecause pawns move forward — Black pawns move downward? No!\n\nCritical point: **Black pawns move *down* the board** — from higher ranks to lower.\n\nStandard chess: White moves up (rank