hing.\n- **B: e4-f3 / g2 / h1**: These retreat the bishop toward White’s side but don’t achieve much; h1 is empty, so only positional gain if preparing zugzwang.\n- **B: e4-d5 / c6 / b7 / a8**: Advancing up the diagonal; d5 is central, c6 threatens b7 and supports invasion, b7 attacks a8 and pressures material; a8 is deep but may overextend.\n- **K: f6-e5**: Centralizes the king and supports advancement or control of key central squares; however, it may walk into checks or forks later.\n- **K: f6-e6 / e7 / f5 / f7 / g6 / g7**: Various king moves; g7 is safer, e7 centralizes, f5 approaches the g5-pawn.\n- **P: a3-a2**: Pushes pawn toward promotion, getting closer to queening; a2 is one step from a1=Q.\n- **P: g3-g2**: Similarly pushes toward g1=Q; g2 is very close to promotion.\n- **P: g5-g4**: Pushes the pawn forward, but this is White’s pawn — wait! This move is in *Black's* valid move list, but g5 has PBF1 — that’s a Black pawn? Wait: g5 has PBF1 → Black pawn on g5? But earlier we see g3 has PBF1 too? Conflict.\n\nWait: Looking back:\n\n- Row 5: g5 has PBF1 → Black pawn on g5\n- Row 3: g3 has PBF1 → another Black pawn?\n\nBut pawns can’t occupy same file unless one moved… but both are marked PBF1 — both Black pawns?\n\nYes: g5 and g3 both have PBF1 → meaning Black has pawns on g5 and g3? That implies g4 is empty.\n\nSo **P: g5-g4** is a valid move: advancing the g5 pawn to g4.\n\nBut g3-g2 is *also* a valid move — so two separate pawns.\n\nBut then g-file has pawns on g5 and g3 — so yes, possible if no capture occurred.\n\nBut now: **P: g3-g2** advances a pawn one step from queening — extremely valuable.\n\nSimilarly, **P: a3-a2** does the same.\n\nBut **g2-square** is empty, so g3-g2 is legal.\n\nAnd after g3-g2, next move g2-g1=Q is possible.\n\nSame with a2-a1=Q.\n\nHowever, **P: g3-g2** gets us closer to promotion — and faster if supported.\n\nBut let’s compare:\n\n- a3-a2: still two moves from promotion (a2, then a1=Q)\n- g3-g2: one move from g2, then g1=Q — so next move is g2-g1=q\n\nWait: g3-g2 is one push, then on next turn g2-g1=Q.\n\nSame for a3-a2: then a2-a1=Q.\n\nBut perhaps g-file is less obstructed?\n\nBut more importantly: **my bishop on e4 can go to c2**, attacking b1 — which is currently empty, but if White ever castles or tucks king there, it's dangerous. But White king is on b3 — already out!\n\nb3 is exposed — and **B: e4-c2** pins or threatens the king if no escape.\n\nBut king is on b3 — c2 is adjacent diagonally? b3 to c2 is diagonal — so bishop on c2 attacks b3 directly!\n\nYes! **B: e4-c2** puts the White king in check!\n\nThat’s critical.\n\nBecause b3 is occupied by KWF1 — the White king.\n\nSo **B: e4-c2** delivers check.\n\nCan the king escape? From b3, king can go to a3, a4, b4, c4, c3 — but:\n- a3: occupied by Black pawn (a3: PBF1) — blocked\n- b4: empty?\nLooking at board:\n- b4: no piece shown — empty\n- c3: empty?\n- c4: empty?\n- c2: where bishop moves to — so square attacked\n\nSo from b3, White king can go to:\n- a3: occupied by Black pawn — no\n- a4: empty? yes, no piece — probably allowed\n- b4: empty — yes\n- c3: empty — yes\n- c4: empty — yes\n\nSo king has escape routes: a4, b4, c3, c4.\n\nSo check is not mate.\n\nBut still, **delivering check with tempo** is powerful — especially because it gains time and may force White into weakening moves.\n\nAdditionally, after Bc2+, if White plays Ka4, then Ba6# might be possible? Not immediately — but long-term pressure.\n\nAlternatively, **P: g3-g2** promotes next move — very fast win if unhindered.\n\nBut White may have counterplay.\n\nBut let’s assess urgency:\n\nIf I play **P: g3-g2**, next move I play g2-g1=Q, promoting to queen — likely winning unless White checkmates first.\n\nBut currently, White cannot deliver any immediate threat — no pieces developed, no attack.\n\nWhite’s king is exposed, and I have multiple paths to advantage.\n\nBut **B: e4-c2+** gives immediate check, disrupts White’s coordination, and may win material or lead 