te: if I push g3-g2, White may respond by Kb4 or something, but still can't stop promotion.\n\nBut also, **the pawn on g5 can push g5-g4**, supporting potential g4-g3-g2 push — but that would be redundant if g3 already pushed.\n\nBut having two pawns on g3 and g5 is fine — but pushing g5-g4 doesn’t help immediate promotion.\n\nIn fact, **P: g5-g4** is less urgent than **P: g3-g2**.\n\nSo best move is **P: g3-g2** — one step from queening.\n\nBut wait: is g2 really safe?\n\ng2 is not attacked by any White piece — White has no pieces except king on b3 and pawns.\n\nNo bishop, knight, rook.\n\nSo no one attacks g2.\n\nThus, **g3-g2 is safe and leads to unavoidable promotion**.\n\nTherefore, it should be prioritized.\n\nBut what about **B: e4-c2+**? It gives check and may win faster?\n\nSuppose:\n\n1. ... B: e4-c2+  \nWhite must move king: say Kb4  \nThen I can play ...g3-g2! Next move g2-g1=Q — still winning.\n\nBut I could have played g3-g2 *first*, same result.\n\nBut by giving check first, I lose a tempo — because after check, White moves, then I push.\n\nWhereas if I push g3-g2 immediately, same thing: White moves, then I prepare promotion.\n\nBut actually, the number of moves to promotion is same.\n\nBut **by checking first, I might distract White**, but promotion is more direct.\n\nMoreover, after Bc2+, if White ignores and does something silly, but still — I still need to promote.\n\nBut here’s a key point: **after g3-g2, my threat is immediate: next move I promote**.\n\nWhite must respond — but even if they try to create counterplay, they can’t stop it.\n\nWhereas Bc2+ forces a move, but doesn’t create an unstoppable threat.\n\nSo **g3-g2 creates a decisive, unavoidable threat of promotion**.\n\nTherefore, it maximizes winning chances.\n\nSimilarly, **a3-a2** also threatens promotion, but no more urgently than g3-g2.\n\nBut g-file might be closer to center or offer better queen activity after promotion — but negligible.\n\nBut both are good.\n\nBut **g3-g2** is listed, so valid.\n\nNow, is there any risk?\n\nIf I play g3-g2, and White plays Kb4, then I play g2-g1=Q.\n\nThen I have a queen — game over.\n\nEven if White tries to push a pawn, no danger.\n\nAlternatively, if I play Bc2+, White plays Kb4, then I play g3-g2 — same position, but I used an extra move.\n\nSo **g3-g2 is more efficient**.\n\nTherefore, best move is **P: g3-g2**.\n\nAnswer: P: g3-g2"}
