uld be **ignored**.\n- P: a3-a2 – Pushes a3 pawn one step, but irrelevant to the current crisis.\n\nCritical Insight: The only way to prevent White from freely promoting and then using a new queen to dominate is to control the promotion square. B: e4-g6 prepares to capture on g1 after promotion, unless White defends it.\n\nBut wait: After B: e4-g6, if White plays g2-g1=Q, then Black can play Bxg1, removing the newly promoted piece. That would leave White with just a king vs. Black’s king and bishop—a theoretically drawn endgame (insufficient mating material). So this is Black’s best chance: trade off the promoted piece into a draw.\n\nHowever, if Black does nothing active like B: e4-g6, White promotes safely and keeps the queen, leading to a winning position.\n\nThus, B: e4-g6 is the only move that actively challenges the promotion.\n\nReason: The move B: e4-g6 targets the g1 square, threatening to capture the pawn upon promotion. It gives Black the best practical chance to force a draw by eliminating White’s promoted piece. All other moves allow White to promote unimpeded, leading to a likely loss.\n\nAnswer: B: e4-g6'}
