\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |QBF1|    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a3-a2, K: f6-e5, K: f6-e6, K: f6-e7, K: f6-f5, K: f6-f7, K: f6-g7, Q: g1-f1, Q: g1-e1, Q: g1-d1, Q: g1-c1, Q: g1-b1, Q: g1-a1, Q: g1-f2, Q: g1-e3, Q: g1-d4, Q: g1-c5, Q: g1-b6, Q: g1-a7, Q: g1-g2, Q: g1-g3, Q: g1-g4, Q: g1-h1, Q: g1-h2, P: g5-g4, B: g6-f5, B: g6-e4, B: g6-d3, B: g6-c2, B: g6-b1, B: g6-h5, B: g6-f7, B: g6-e8, B: g6-h7]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and the board is nearly empty. My king is on f6, exposed but not in check. The only active pieces I have are the king (KBF1) on f6, a bishop (BBF1) on g6, and a pawn (PBF1) on g5. Additionally, I have a queen (QBF1) on g1. White has a single king on b4 and a pawn on a3, which is one step away from promotion. My main threats are the White king’s proximity to my pawns and the danger of White promoting their a-pawn. However, White’s king is currently close to my queenside, while my own king is central and slightly vulnerable.\n\nThe most urgent issue is that White's king is very close to my a3-pawn and can capture it soon if not deterred. Conversely, my pieces are far from White's back rank, but I do have long-range pieces like the queen and bishop that can exert pressure.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **King moves (f6-e5/e6/e7/f5/f7/g7)**: Moving the king toward the center or up could help support the bishop or queen activity, but it doesn’t directly threaten anything. e5 might be useful to approach the center, but it also walks into potential stalemate risks later if not careful.\n- **Pawn a3-a2**: This advances my pawn one step closer to promotion. Crucially, after a3-a2, the next move will be a2-a1=Q/R/B/N, giving me a new queen unless stopped. This is a strong candidate because it forces White to respond—either capture the pawn or allow me to promote.\n- **Queen moves (g1 to various squares)**: The queen can become very powerful. Notably:\n  - Qg1-a7 threatens the a3 pawn? No — wait, a3 is mine. More importantly, Qg1-a7 puts pressure on the a-file and could potentially stop White’s king from advancing safely.\n  - Qg1-b1 attacks b4 square — the white king! That would put the White king in check from a distance. Wait: b1 is two files left and one down from g1 — yes, possible via diagonal? g1 to b1 is along the first rank — horizontal. Yes, queen can move horizontally. So Qg1-b1 is a legal move and delivers check to Kb4!\n    - After Qg1-b1+, White must respond: Kb4-c5, c4, a4, or a3 (all except a3 are safe). But critically, this checks the king and gains tempo.\n- **Bishop moves (g6 to various)**: The bishop can target key light squares. For example:\n  - Bg6-f7 or e8 could aim at the back rank.\n  - Bg6-c2 targets a2/a3? Not immediately useful.\n  - Bg6-d3 or e4 may support central control, but with little material, effect is limited.\n- **Pawn g5-g4**: Loses time; no immediate benefit.\n\nKey Insight:\n- Promoting the a-pawn is critical. a3-a2 prepares a2-a1=Q next move, which would give Black a decisive material advantage unless prevented.\n- However, White can play Kb4xa3 on their next turn if we don’t act — so we must either protect the pawn or force them to deal with something more urgent.\n- If we play **Qg1-b1+**, checking the White king, then White must move their king, and cannot capture our a3-pawn this turn. Then next move we can play a3-a2, followed by a2-a1=Q.\n- Alternatively, if we play a3-a2 now, White can simply ignore it and play Kb4xa3, capturing the pawn. Then we lose the pawn without compensation.\n\nTherefore, **checking the king first with Qg1-b1** is better than pushing the pawn.\n\nBut wait — is Qg1-b1 even possible? Let's verify:\n\n- Queen on g1 (file g, rank 1), moving to b1 (file b, rank 1): same rank, horizontal move. Yes, allowed. And White king is on b4 — so when queen goes to b1, it attacks b4 vertically? No! b1 and b4 are on the same file (b), not the same rank.\n\nWait — correction: g1 to b1 is along rank 1 — so it's horizontal. From g1 to b1: moves left 5 squares on the first rank. Then the queen ends up on b1. The White king is on b4 — same file! So yes, queen on b1 attacks b4 vertically. So **Qg1-b1 does attack the White king on b4**, and since it's a direct line along the b-file, it is indeed **check**!\n\nYes! So Qg1-b1+ gives check.\n\nThis is a strong move — it forces the White king to move, prevents him from capturing our a3 pawn this turn, and gains initiative.\n\nAfter Qg1-b1+, White must move Kb4 to a4, a3, c3, c4, or c5 (depending on availability). All are forced, and we gain tempo.\n\nThen next move, we can push a3-a2, preparing promotion.\n\nAlternatively, if we push a3-a2 now, White plays Kb4xa3 and captures it — bad for us.\n\nThus, **prioritize the check**.\n\nOther queen moves like Qg1-a1 or Qg1-c1 also attack b1 or d1 but not the king. Only Qg1-b1 delivers check.\n\nHence, best move: **Q: g1-b1**\n\nReason: It delivers check to the White king, forcing it to move and losing time for White, preventing the capture of the a3-pawn next turn, and allowing Black to advance the pawn toward promotion with tempo.\n\nAnswer: Q: g1-b1"}
