\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a3-a2, Q: f5-e4, Q: f5-d3, Q: f5-c2, Q: f5-b1, Q: f5-e5, Q: f5-d5, Q: f5-c5, Q: f5-b5, Q: f5-a5, Q: f5-e6, Q: f5-d7, Q: f5-c8, Q: f5-f4, Q: f5-f3, Q: f5-f2, Q: f5-f1, Q: f5-g4, Q: f5-h3, K: f6-e5, K: f6-e6, K: f6-e7, K: f6-f7, K: f6-g7, P: g5-g4, B: g6-h5, B: g6-f7, B: g6-e8, B: g6-h7]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: Black is in a highly advantageous position. The white king is dangerously exposed on b6, with very limited mobility and no immediate protection. The black queen on f5 exerts strong pressure along the 5th rank and diagonal, and can reach deep into white’s side. The black bishop on g6 adds control over key light squares, and the black king on f6 is relatively safe but could be at risk if passive. White has no active pieces visible, and their pawn on a3 is about to promote but will take several moves (a3-a2-a1=Q). Black must act decisively to exploit the exposed white king.\n\nEvaluation:\n- Q: f5-e4 – Threatens check on e4, but not immediately decisive.\n- Q: f5-d7 – Delivers check! Queen takes on d7 attacks the white king directly: Qd7+ forces Kc6 or Kb7, but after Qd7+, White king must move to c6 (only legal), then Bf7+ forks king and queen or leads to mate.\n- Q: f5-c8 – Also delivers check: Qc8+ Kb7, then potential follow-up with Ba6+ or Qc7+ leading to quick mate.\n- B: g6-f7 – Attacks e6 and potentially supports a mating net, but less urgent than queen checks.\n- Q: f5-b1 – Long-range threat, but too slow.\n- K: f6-e5 – King centralization, but risky and unnecessary when we can deliver check now.\n\nThe most forcing and decisive moves are **Q: f5-d7** and **Q: f5-c8**, both delivering check and threatening immediate mate.\n\nBetween them:\n- Q: f5-d7+ Kc6, then Bf7+! forking king and queen — winning material and leading to mate shortly.\n- Q: f5-c8+ Kb7, then Qc7+ Ka6, Qb6+ Ka7, Ra8# — but no rook, so less direct.\n\nBut **Q: f5-d7** leads to faster collapse due to the discovered battery with the bishop.\n\nHowever, note: **Q: f5-d7** is *not* in the valid move list. Wait — checking again.\n\nValid moves listed:  \n[... Q: f5-d7 ...] → Yes, it *is* included.\n\nBut wait: from f5 to d7 is two files left, two ranks up — that’s a diagonal move? f5 → e6 → d7: yes, diagonal, so queen can do that.\n\nSo **Q: f5-d7** is both legal and devastating.\n\nReason: Q: f5-d7+ forces the white king to c6 (only escape), then the black bishop on g6 can play Bf7+ (forking king and queen) next move, winning the queen or delivering mate shortly thereafter. This sequence leads to an immediate and decisive advantage.\n\nAnswer: Q: f5-d7'}
