\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a3-a2, Q: d7-c6, Q: d7-b5, Q: d7-a4, Q: d7-c7, Q: d7-b7, Q: d7-a7, Q: d7-c8, Q: d7-d6, Q: d7-d5, Q: d7-d4, Q: d7-d3, Q: d7-d2, Q: d7-d1, Q: d7-d8, Q: d7-e6, Q: d7-f5, Q: d7-g4, Q: d7-h3, Q: d7-e7, Q: d7-f7, Q: d7-g7, Q: d7-h7, Q: d7-e8, K: f6-e5, K: f6-e6, K: f6-e7, K: f6-f5, K: f6-f7, K: f6-g7, P: g5-g4, B: g6-f5, B: g6-e4, B: g6-d3, B: g6-c2, B: g6-b1, B: g6-h5, B: g6-f7, B: g6-e8, B: g6-h7]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and currently have a strong positional advantage due to the placement of my queen on d7, which is centralized and exerts pressure across multiple ranks, files, and diagonals. My king is on f6, unusually far advanced, but not immediately under threat. White’s king is on e5 (KWF1), exposed in the center, and there is a white pawn on g5 (PBF1). My bishop on g6 (BBF1) adds control over key light squares. The board is otherwise sparse, with few pieces remaining, suggesting an endgame or near-endgame scenario.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **Q: d7-d8** – Threatens immediate checkmate if the king cannot escape, since it attacks e8 and possibly delivers check. However, Kf6 is adjacent to d8; need to assess if this checks the king.\n  - d8 is one diagonal away from f6 — d8 to f6 is two squares down and two right — not adjacent. So Qd8 does *not* attack f6. No check. But it pressures the back rank.\n- **Q: d7-c8** – Attacks b8, a8, and crucially, the square e8, but also eyes the king via c8-e6 diagonal? Not directly threatening.\n- **Q: d7-e8** – Directly captures material if anything were there, but e8 is empty. More importantly, e8 is adjacent to f6 — so Qe8 would place the black queen next to the black king? That’s a self-attack — illegal. Cannot move to e8.\n  - Actually, e8 is adjacent to f6? f6 → e8 is one left, two up — knight move, not adjacent. Adjacent squares to f6 are e5, e6, e7, f5, f7, g5, g6, g7. e8 is not adjacent. So Qe8 is safe.\n  - But more importantly: does Qe8 threaten the white king on e5? From e8, queen can go vertically down the e-file. e5 is on e-file. So yes — **Q: d7-e8** puts the white king in check!\n  - Is e5 attacked by e8? Yes — same file. Queen on e8 controls e1–e8. So Qe8+ checks the white king.\n  - Can White escape? King on e5, checked by queen on e8. Flight squares: d5, d6, e4, f4, f5, d4, f6 — but f6 is occupied by our own king. d5: empty? Yes. d6: empty? Yes. e4: empty? Yes. f5: occupied by PBF1 (white pawn). So king can go to d5, d6, e4, f4 (if exists), etc. But importantly — **it's check**, forcing White to respond.\n  - Also, after Qe8+, if White responds poorly, we may follow up with tactics.\n\n- **Q: d7-g4** – Attacks g4, potentially eyeing h3 or supporting future action, but less urgent.\n- **B: g6-f5** – Develops bishop, attacks e4/d3, but passive compared to queen action.\n- **P: a3-a2** – Pushes pawn toward promotion, but very slow; a2 is still far from a8.\n- **K: f6-e5** – Captures the white king’s square? e5 is occupied by KWF1 — cannot capture your own king. Illegal. Wait — no: different colors. KWF1 is *white*. So e5 has white king. Black king cannot move into e5 — it would be adjacent to enemy king? No — kings cannot be adjacent. f6 to e5 is one diagonal — adjacent. So **illegal move**. All king moves must avoid adjacency.\n  - Legal king moves: e6, e7, f5, f7, g7 — all must not be adjacent to e5.\n    - e6: adjacent to e5? yes — same file, one apart → adjacent → illegal.\n    - e7: e7 and e5 are two apart — not adjacent → legal.\n    - f5: adjacent to e5? yes (diagonally) → illegal.\n    - f7: f7 and e5 — one up, one left — knight move apart? No: difference is Δfile=1, Δrank=2 → not adjacent. Adjacent means differ by 1 in any direction. So f7 and e5: |f-e|=1, |7-5|=2 → not adjacent → legal.\n    - g7: |g-e|=2, |7-5|=2 → not adjacent → legal.\n    - e5: same square as white king → impossible.\n    - So valid king moves: **K: f6-e7, K: f6-f7, K: f6-g7** only. Others like e5, e6, f5 are adjacent to e5 → illegal.\n\nThus, king options are limited and risky.\n\nBack to queen: **Q: d7-e8** gives check and forces White to move the king, losing tempo. It centralizes the queen and increases pressure. This seems strongest.\n\nAlso consider **Q: d7-h3** — long diagonal, but less immediate impact.\n\nBut **Q: d7-e8+** is a direct check, highly forcing, and may lead to gaining material or creating promotion threats.\n\nReason: Delivering check with the queen maximizes initiative. Qe8+ forces the white king to move, potentially into a worse position, while activating the most powerful piece. It improves piece activity and creates immediate tactical tension. Other moves are slower or less threatening.\n\nAnswer: Q: d7-e8"}
