n e8\n- Black pawn on g5 and a3\n\nWhite has no other pieces visible — only the king on a6. That suggests White is completely lost, possibly due to a blunder or handicap.\n\nMy Black forces are very strong: queen on e8, bishop on g6, king on f6, pawns on g5 and a3. White has only a lone king on a6, with no defenders.\n\nBlack to move.\n\nNow evaluating threats and opportunities:\n\n- White king on a6 is extremely vulnerable.\n- Black can deliver checkmate using queen and bishop or king support.\n- Immediate threat: Can I attack a6?\n\nLet’s evaluate key moves:\n\n1. Qe8-a4+? But a4 is not adjacent to a6. Queen on a4 would control b5, c4, etc., but not directly threaten a6 unless supported.\n\nBut wait: Queen from e8 to a4 is possible (diagonal if aligned). e8 to a4 is along the same diagonal (e8–d7–c6–b5–a4)? No: e8 to a4 is *not* diagonal. It's a rank or file issue.\n\ne8 to a4: file change from e to a (5 left), rank from 8 to 4 (4 down) — so yes, it's a diagonal move (5 left, 4 down) — not equal, so not diagonal. Invalid for queen.\n\nWait — this reveals a problem: **Only valid queen moves are straight lines**.\n\nSo let’s correct: From e8, queen can move:\n\n- Horizontally: e-file\n- Vertically: 8th rank\n- Diagonally: a4–h1 and h5–a8 diagonals?\n\ne8 is on:\n- The e-file (vertical)\n- The 8th rank (horizontal)\n- Diagonal a4–h1? a4, b5, c6, d7, e8 → YES, that’s a diagonal.\n- Other diagonal: h5–a12? h5, g6, f7, e8 → YES.\n\nSo queen on e8 can go:\n- Along e-file: e7, e6, ..., e1\n- Along 8th rank: d8, c8, b8, a8, f8, g8, h8\n- Diagonal a4–h1: d7, c6, b5, a4 (and backwards: f7, g6, h5)\n- Diagonal h5–a12: f7, g6, h5 (same as above)\n\nWait — both diagonals from e8 go through f7 and d7, then g6 and c6, etc.\n\nSo valid queen moves include:\n- e8-d7 (diagonal)\n- e8-c6 (d7, c6)\n- e8-b5\n- e8-a4\n- e8-f7\n- e8-g6? But g6 has BBF1 (Black bishop) — occupied by own piece, so cannot go there.\n\nSimilarly, e8-f8, g8, h8 (along 8th rank)\ne8-d8, c8, b8, a8\ne8-e7, e6, ..., e1\n\nNow, can the queen reach a6? a6 is on a-file, 6th rank.\n\nFrom e8, can queen go to a6? That would require moving 4 files left and 2 ranks down — not horizontal, vertical, or diagonal. Not a straight line. So **no**, queen cannot go directly to a6.\n\nBut what about c6? c6 is on the a4–h1 diagonal. From c6, queen could later go to b5, a6?\n\nYes: from c6, queen can go to b5, a4 (diagonal), but also — wait — c6 to a6 is along the 6th rank? Yes!\n\nSo:\n- Qe8–c6 attacks the entire 6th rank, including a6!\n\nAnd White king is on a6 — currently not in check.\n\nSo Qe8–c6 puts White king in check.\n\nMoreover, can the king escape?\n\nKing on a6:\n- Can go to a5, a7, b5, b6, b7\nBut:\n- a5: is it attacked?\n- Queen on c6 controls a6, b6, c6, d6, e6, f6, g6, h6 (rank)\nAlso controls a4, b5, c6, d7, e8 (diagonal)\nSo:\n- b6: on same rank → controlled\n- b5: on same diagonal → controlled\n\nSo king cannot go to b5 or b6.\n\na5: file a, rank 5 — not on same rank or diagonal from c6? \n- a5 is below a6 — is it attacked by queen on c6? Only if on same line.\n\nQueen on c6 controls:\n- Rank 6: all files\n- File c: all ranks\n- Diagonals: a4–h1 and b7–h1?\n\nDiagonals from c6:\n- c6–b5–a4 (one diagonal)\n- c6–d7–e8\n- c6–b7–a8\n- c6–d5–e4–f3–g2–h1\n\nSo a5 is NOT on any of these.\n\nTherefore, a5 is **not** attacked by queen on c6.\n\nSo king could go a6–a5 to escape.\n\nBut we have other pieces.\n\nWe have:\n- Black pawn on g5\n- Black bishop on g6\n- Black king on f6\n- Black pawn on a3\n\nNo other immediate attackers near a6.\n\nBut after Qc6+, Ka5, can we follow up?\n\nQc6–b5#? Because b5 is adjacent to a5, and queen on b5 would attack a5.\n\nYes! After:\n1. Qe8–c6+ Ka6–a5\n2. Qc6–b5#\n\nCheckmate: king cannot go back to a6 (controlled), b6 (controlled), b5 (occupied), a4 (queen might control?), and no other squares.\n\nWait — after Qb5, king on a5 is attacked, and:\n- a4: is it safe? a4 is on the same diagonal (a4–h1) which queen controls from b5? b5–a4 is one step